        "data": {
            "key": "95P4JF2U",
            "version": 48,
            "parentItem": "W9H6FLGV",
            "itemType": "note",
            "note": "<div data-schema-version=\"8\"><p>This paper uses a fair algorithm to make LLM’s prediction on classification tasks related to sensitive groups more fair. Specifically, given the task input, the model first make prediction, where we can access the logit/probability of each ourput (e.g. male 0.6, female 0.4, or white 0.3, asian 0.2, etc.). The paper then fits them to the label and sensitive groups’ attributes with logistic regression and obtain the calibration. Finally, given the calibration, the fair algorithm take it as probability vector <span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0)\">input to fair-ML method</span>. This fair algorithm can be instantiated with multiple in-processing or post-processing algorithms. <span style=\"color: rgb(0, 0, 0)\">Across several datasets, fairness criteria, and both open and closed LLMs, the paper shows that this approach gives strong fairness–accuracy tradeoffs, especially in low-data settings where the low-dimensional extracted features help reduce sample complexity.</span></p>\n</div>",
            "tags": [],
            "relations": {},
            "dateAdded": "2026-04-02T15:02:46Z",
            "dateModified": "2026-04-02T16:32:03Z"
        }
    },
